PATCH: HalGPIO — report the completed hold on the release frame

Prepared in the crosspoint-reader firmware tree (tools/patches/README.md explains
why it cannot live there). Not an mbox: apply with `git apply`, not `git am`.

Target repo: https://github.com/crosspoint-reader/crosspoint-simulator
File:        src/HalGPIO.cpp
Base:        ed31bd9 ("feat: support firmware web server output"), branch main
Apply with:  git apply -p1 <this file>   (from the simulator repo root)

THE DEFECT
----------
HalGPIO::getHeldTime() only counts buttons that are down at the moment it is
called:

    src/HalGPIO.cpp:584-598   for (i) if (keystate[i] || syntheticButtonDown[i]) ...
                              return maxHeld;   // 0 when nothing is down

The release frame is by definition the frame on which the button is no longer
down. Both release paths latch releasedThisFrame[] before the firmware's loop()
runs, and both leave the button reading "up" to that loop: the SDL KEYUP branch
(src/HalGPIO.cpp:508-516) because SDL's own key state has already gone up, and
the synthetic KeyUp branch (src/HalGPIO.cpp:385-388) because it clears
syntheticButtonDown[]. So every firmware check of the shape

    wasReleased(BUTTON) && getHeldTime() <compare> THRESHOLD

reads 0 in the simulator and the wrong branch is taken. Three confirmed cases in
crosspoint-reader (paths relative to that repo):

  * src/activities/home/FileBrowserActivity.cpp:209+230
    `wasReleased(Confirm)` then `getHeldTime() >= GO_HOME_MS` — the long-press
    "Delete?" prompt can never fire in the simulator (false negative).

  * src/activities/home/FileBrowserActivity.cpp:278-280
    `wasReleased(Back)` then `getHeldTime() < GO_HOME_MS` — after a long Back
    hold has already jumped to the root folder, the release reads 0, passes the
    "short press" test and calls onGoHome(). Simulator-only false positive: the
    device correctly reports the completed hold here, so this is NOT a firmware
    bug and must not be "fixed" in the firmware.

  * src/activities/reader/EpubReaderActivity.cpp:542
    `getHeldTime() > SKIP_HOLD_MS` evaluated after ReaderUtils::detectPageTurn(),
    which is release-triggered whenever SETTINGS.longPressButtonBehavior != OFF
    (src/activities/reader/ReaderUtils.h:49-58) — chapter skip is dead in the
    simulator.

The `isPressed(BUTTON) && getHeldTime()` shape is NOT affected and is correct
today; this patch deliberately leaves that path byte-for-byte identical.

THE DEVICE BEHAVIOUR IT MUST MATCH
----------------------------------
freeink-sdk libs/hardware/InputManager/src/InputManager.cpp:405-412

    unsigned long InputManager::getHeldTime() const {
      if (currentState > 0) return millis() - buttonPressStart;   // still down
      return buttonPressFinish - buttonPressStart;                // completed
    }

with buttonPressStart stamped when a button goes down while nothing was down and
buttonPressFinish stamped when the last button comes up (InputManager.cpp:
242-252). The completed value persists until the next hold ends, so a caller one
or two frames after the release still sees it.

THE FIX
-------
Latch the completed hold at each release (recordButtonRelease()) and return it
from getHeldTime() when no button is down. `completedHoldFresh` is cleared in
beginFrame() so that when several buttons are released in the same frame the
longest of them is reported, which is what the device's chord-span value comes
out as in that case. clearButtonState() (deep sleep) clears the latch, matching
the state loss of a real wake.

Known deviation, deliberately not chased: for an OVERLAPPING chord where the
first-pressed button is released first (UP down, BACK down, UP up, BACK up), the
device reports the whole chord span from UP's press while this patch reports
BACK's own press-to-release span. Measured on the host: 796 ms here vs the
device's ~1100 ms for that trace. Closing that gap needs an authoritative
"is anything else down" latch; on SDL that latch can be left stuck by a focus
loss that swallows the KEYUP, and a stuck latch would make getHeldTime() return
an ever-growing value with nothing pressed — a far worse failure than the
residual inaccuracy. No firmware call site depends on the difference.

HOW TO TEST
-----------
Build the simulator project as usual, then use the built-in synthetic input
script (src/HalGPIO.cpp:290-297, CROSSPOINT_SIM_INPUT_SCRIPT="<atMs>:<KEY>[:<holdMs>];..."):

  1. Long Back hold in the file browser — before: drops to Home on release;
     after: stays in the browser at the root folder.
       CROSSPOINT_SIM_INPUT_SCRIPT="1500:ESCAPE:1500;6000:QUIT" .pio/build/simulator/program

  2. Long Confirm hold on a book — before: opens the book; after: shows the
     "Delete?" confirmation.
       CROSSPOINT_SIM_INPUT_SCRIPT="1500:RETURN:1500;6000:QUIT" .pio/build/simulator/program

  3. Short taps must be unchanged (`:80` holds), and continuous list scrolling
     (hold Up/Down) must still repeat — that path uses the isPressed shape.

Verified while preparing this patch: the translation unit was compiled on macOS
(clang++ -std=gnu++2a -Wall -Wextra, zero diagnostics, patched and pristine) and
linked into a probe that drives the real HalGPIO with the synthetic script:

  script 300:ESCAPE:1500  before: running=1488 release_frame=0     after: running=1490 release_frame=1503
  script 300:ESCAPE:80    before: running=72   release_frame=0     after: running=69   release_frame=82
  script 300:RETURN:1200  before: running=1180 release_frame=0     after: running=1189 release_frame=1202
  long 1500 then short 60 before: release_frame=0                  after: release_frame=59  (no stale hold)

diff --git a/src/HalGPIO.cpp b/src/HalGPIO.cpp
index f9d5850..2712a14 100644
--- a/src/HalGPIO.cpp
+++ b/src/HalGPIO.cpp
@@ -55,6 +55,36 @@ static unsigned long buttonPressTime[NUM_BUTTONS] = {};
 static bool syntheticButtonDown[NUM_BUTTONS] = {};
 static bool simulatorSleepRequested = false;
 
+// Completed hold of the most recent release, mirroring InputManager's
+// `buttonPressFinish - buttonPressStart`. The device reports the finished hold
+// once every button is up (InputManager::getHeldTime), so a release-frame check
+// such as `wasReleased(BTN_BACK) && getHeldTime() < GO_HOME_MS` sees the real
+// duration there. Without this the host returned 0 on the release frame, which
+// inverted every such check. Latched at release and kept until the next one, as
+// on device; `completedHoldFresh` is cleared per frame so several buttons
+// released in the same frame report the longest hold rather than the last one.
+static unsigned long completedHoldMs = 0;
+static bool completedHoldFresh = false;
+
+// Latches the release edge and the hold it completed. Shared by the SDL KEYUP
+// path and the CROSSPOINT_SIM_INPUT_SCRIPT KeyUp path so both report held time
+// the same way.
+static void recordButtonRelease(int button) {
+  const unsigned long now = SDL_GetTicks();
+  releasedThisFrame[button] = true;
+
+  // buttonPressTime is 0 when the matching key-down was never seen (key held
+  // before the window gained focus, or state cleared by deep sleep). Report 0
+  // rather than an epoch-sized hold.
+  const unsigned long pressedAt = buttonPressTime[button];
+  const unsigned long held =
+      (pressedAt > 0 && now >= pressedAt) ? now - pressedAt : 0;
+  if (!completedHoldFresh || held > completedHoldMs) {
+    completedHoldMs = held;
+    completedHoldFresh = true;
+  }
+}
+
 namespace {
 
 struct TouchState {
@@ -383,7 +413,7 @@ void processSyntheticEvents() {
       buttonPressTime[event.button] = SDL_GetTicks();
       break;
     case SyntheticAction::KeyUp:
-      releasedThisFrame[event.button] = true;
+      recordButtonRelease(event.button);
       syntheticButtonDown[event.button] = false;
       break;
     case SyntheticAction::TouchDown:
@@ -417,6 +447,10 @@ static void clearButtonState() {
     buttonPressTime[i] = 0;
     syntheticButtonDown[i] = false;
   }
+  // A hold completed before deep sleep must not be visible to the first frame
+  // after wake — on device the state is gone with the reboot.
+  completedHoldMs = 0;
+  completedHoldFresh = false;
   touchState = {};
   homeKeyDown = false;
   homeKeyPressedThisFrame = false;
@@ -466,6 +500,9 @@ void HalGPIO::beginFrame() {
     pressedThisFrame[i] = false;
     releasedThisFrame[i] = false;
   }
+  // completedHoldMs itself persists (the device keeps the finished hold until
+  // the next one); only the per-frame "already latched" marker is cleared.
+  completedHoldFresh = false;
   touchState.pressedThisFrame = false;
   touchState.releasedThisFrame = false;
   touchState.activityThisFrame = false;
@@ -512,7 +549,7 @@ void HalGPIO::update() {
       }
       int btn = scancodeToButton(e.key.keysym.scancode);
       if (btn >= 0) {
-        releasedThisFrame[btn] = true;
+        recordButtonRelease(btn);
       }
     } else if (e.type == SDL_MOUSEBUTTONDOWN &&
                e.button.button == SDL_BUTTON_LEFT) {
@@ -582,19 +619,30 @@ bool HalGPIO::wasAnyReleased() const {
 }
 
 unsigned long HalGPIO::getHeldTime() const {
-  // Return the longest held time among all currently pressed buttons
+  // Mirrors InputManager::getHeldTime(): the running hold while a button is
+  // still down, otherwise the hold that the last release completed. Returning 0
+  // once everything was up (the old behaviour) broke every firmware check of
+  // the form `wasReleased(X) && getHeldTime() <compare> N`, because the release
+  // frame is exactly the frame on which no button is down any more.
   unsigned long now = SDL_GetTicks();
   unsigned long maxHeld = 0;
+  bool anyDown = false;
   const uint8_t *state = SDL_GetKeyboardState(NULL);
   for (int i = 0; i < NUM_BUTTONS; i++) {
-    if ((state[buttonScancode[i]] || syntheticButtonDown[i]) &&
-        buttonPressTime[i] > 0) {
-      unsigned long held = now - buttonPressTime[i];
-      if (held > maxHeld)
-        maxHeld = held;
+    if (state[buttonScancode[i]] || syntheticButtonDown[i]) {
+      anyDown = true;
+      if (buttonPressTime[i] > 0) {
+        unsigned long held = now - buttonPressTime[i];
+        if (held > maxHeld)
+          maxHeld = held;
+      }
     }
   }
-  return maxHeld;
+  // Still held: longest held time among the currently pressed buttons, as
+  // before. Only the all-released case changes.
+  if (anyDown)
+    return maxHeld;
+  return completedHoldMs;
 }
 
